INT. COUNTY COURTHOUSE - CRAMPED JURY DELIBERATION ROOM - NIGHT

One table. Five chairs. A radiator that clanks. A pitcher of water gone warm. A single window painted shut.

AUDREY KESSLER, 40s, foreperson, sleeves rolled, stands at a whiteboard. MR. SELLERS, 60s, retired machinist, sits with his arms crossed. FRANKLIN OKORO, 30s, an accountant with a calculator he keeps pressing. MAYBELLE TRUDEAU, 70s, knitting, needles clicking.

AUDREY
All right. Another vote. Hands.

Nobody moves. The radiator clanks.

AUDREY (CONT'D)
Guilty.

Sellers keeps his arms crossed. Franklin presses a key on the calculator. Maybelle's needles never pause.

AUDREY (CONT'D)
Not guilty.

Sellers shifts. Franklin sets the calculator down. The needles click.

AUDREY (CONT'D)
That's the same as the last four.

SELLERS
Then we vote again.

AUDREY
We've voted all night.

SELLERS
I can vote till Easter.

FRANKLIN
The numbers don't add up.

AUDREY
Which numbers?

FRANKLIN
The defendant's timeline. Three minutes missing.

SELLERS
Three minutes isn't a man's life.

FRANKLIN
It is if you're counting.

MAYBELLE
(not looking up)
Everyone counts different, dear.

AUDREY
Maybelle, what do you think?

MAYBELLE
I think the boy looked at his mother when the verdict came up in his head.

AUDREY
The verdict hasn't come up.

MAYBELLE
It will.

Silence. The needles click.

SELLERS
You're going to hang us on a feeling.

MAYBELLE
No. I'll hang us on a fact.

SELLERS
Which?

MAYBELLE
The knot.

AUDREY
What knot?

MAYBELLE
The one in the rope. Exhibit nine. It's a fisherman's bend, not a hangman's. Whoever tied it was left-handed.

FRANKLIN
The defendant is right-handed.

SELLERS
(slowly)
That's not in evidence.

MAYBELLE
It's in the photo. I looked.

Audrey puts down the marker. Sits. The room settles into an uneasy, new quiet. Franklin picks up the calculator again and begins to type, fast.

FRANKLIN
If he's right-handed, and the timeline's off by three minutes...

AUDREY
Then we vote again.

SELLERS
(grudging)
Then we vote again.

Two knocks. The door opens a hand's width. JUDGE ZAPATA, 50s, in robes, tired, leans in.

ZAPATA
The court reporter is leaving. Do you need the night?

AUDREY
We need the night.

ZAPATA
Then I'll tell the bailiff. Good evening.

The door closes.
